SKIP nl: a width of zero (GNU appends its C library's strerror(ERANGE) to the message, and the two hosts word it differently at one coreutils release: 'Numerical result out of range' under glibc and 'Result too large' under Apple's. boreutils prints glibc's, so this case cannot pass on both hosts.)
SKIP nl: a BRE interval, which this nl does not implement (The BRE subset here is ^ $ . * and bracket expressions. An interval \{n,m\}, a group \(…\) or a back reference is matched as the literal characters it is written with, so GNU numbers the 'aa' line and boreutils numbers neither. wolf 0.2.14 has no regular-expression surface to borrow, so the subset is written out rather than wrapped.)
